# Bounded CMA-ES

Bounded CMA-ES combines covariance adaptation with an adaptive quadratic
boundary penalty. It samples in unrestricted model coordinates, clips each
sample into the feasible box for evaluation, and uses the distance moved
by that repair to guide selection. Every reported parameter and objective
value refers to an evaluated point inside the box.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bounded_cma_es/struct.BoundedCmaEs.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function of continuous parameters |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` and a complete box-only `Constraints` declaration |
| Search | Stochastic population search from an initial mean |
| Constraints | Box bounds, including unbounded coordinate sides |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider bounded CMA-ES when derivatives are unavailable, parameters
interact strongly, and the objective must only receive feasible points.
It learns the same full covariance as [CMA-ES](../cma-es/) while handling
minima on box faces and corners without repeatedly rejecting and redrawing
infeasible samples.

[Hansen (2016)](#ref-hansen-2016) describes the CMA core. The boundary
penalty follows [Hansen et al.
(2009)](#ref-hansen-niederberger-guzzella-koumoutsakos-2009), with the
specific adaptation used by the pinned
[`pycma` `BoundPenalty` implementation](https://github.com/CMA-ES/pycma/blob/r4.4.4/cma/boundary_handler.py).

For smooth objectives with reliable gradients, compare
[L-BFGS-B](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs-b/). For a local interpolation model and
an expensive objective, compare [BOBYQA](../../derivative-free/bobyqa/).
Bounded CMA-ES can explore multiple basins, but one finite run does not
certify a global minimum. Compare different seeds and starting means.

## How it works

The CMA distribution remains `N(m, sigma² C)` in unrestricted coordinates.
For each sampled point `x`, the solver constructs its box repair `x_rep`
by clamping each component. It evaluates only `f(x_rep)` and ranks the
sample using

```text
fitness(x) = f(x_rep) + sum(gamma[i] * (x[i] - x_rep[i])²) / n
```

The original, unrepaired point enters mean and covariance adaptation.
This distinction lets a sample's distance outside the box influence the
search model even though the objective never sees that point. The
per-coordinate penalty weights `gamma` start at `1` and adapt from recent
objective spreads, coordinate variances, and the mean's boundary violation.

The solver uses active covariance adaptation, evolution paths, and an
eigendecomposition on every generation, as in plain CMA-ES. The default
population size is `4 + floor(3 ln n)`. Fresh initialization projects the
starting mean into the box before drawing the first population.

The published current point is the clipped mean and its raw objective
value. Population members are also clipped, with raw costs, but retain
the ranking determined by penalized fitness. Their costs can therefore
appear out of numerical order. The historical incumbent compares raw
objective values across these evaluated members and means.

## Example

This objective would minimize at `[2.0, -1.0]` without constraints. The
declared box restricts its first coordinate to `[-1.0, 1.0]`, so the
constrained minimum is `[1.0, -1.0]`, with cost `1.0`. The objective
asserts that every point it receives satisfies the box.

<!-- compile: solver-bounded-cma-es -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoundedCmaEs, BoxBounds, Constrained, ConstraintSet, CostFunction,
    DenseMatrix, Executor,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        assert!((-1.0..=1.0).contains(&x[0]));
        assert!((-2.0..=2.0).contains(&x[1]));
        Ok((x[0] - 2.0).powi(2) + (x[1] + 1.0).powi(2))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let bounds = BoxBounds::new(Some(vec![-1.0, -2.0]), Some(vec![1.0, 2.0]));
    let problem = Constrained::new(
        Quadratic,
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(bounds),
    );
    let solver = BoundedCmaEs::<Vec<f64>, DenseMatrix>::new(42, 0.6)
        .with_absolute_distribution_size_tolerance(1e-10);

    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![0.0, 1.0])
        .max_iter(500)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!((result.best_cost() - 1.0).abs() < 1e-8);
    assert!((result.best_param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.best_param()[1] + 1.0).abs() < 1e-4);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Declare the complete feasible box

Constraints belong to the problem. `Constrained::new` installs the supplied
`ConstraintSet` as its complete declaration, replacing any declaration on
the wrapped objective. You can instead implement `Constraints` directly
with `Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>`.

This solver requires both lower and upper vectors. Represent an unbounded
coordinate side with negative infinity in the lower vector or positive
infinity in the upper vector. Each vector must match the starting mean's
dimension, and each lower bound must be at most its upper bound. NaN,
inverted intervals, and infinities on the wrong side are invalid.
Linear and nonlinear constraint blocks are unsupported by the box-only
solver contract.

### Keep raw objective values separate from search fitness

`result.cost()` and `result.best_cost()` exclude the boundary penalty.
Likewise, published population costs match their clipped points. Use
`penalized_costs()` on a retained solver when inspecting selection order,
and `genotypes()` for the corresponding unrestricted samples.

The live `mean()` also describes unrestricted model coordinates and can
move outside the box after initialization. Use the result's `param()` for
the evaluated, clipped mean. Clipping a model mean can leave the current
point unchanged even while covariance and penalty adaptation continue.
That observation alone is insufficient evidence of convergence.

### Scale the initial distribution and budget full generations

`new(seed, sigma)` requires a finite, strictly positive scale. Optional
`with_stds` sets positive, finite coordinate factors, so the initial
standard deviation in coordinate `i` is `sigma * stds[i]`. Match these
scales to the feasible widths and the distance worth exploring. A very
wide distribution can spend much of its effort on repairs to a small
part of the boundary.

`with_lambda` requires at least four members. Initialization and each
completed generation use `lambda + 1` objective evaluations, including
the repaired mean. The boundary penalty itself requires no additional
objective calls. Executor budgets are checked at iteration boundaries,
so one generation can exceed a remaining evaluation allowance.

### Configure a distribution tolerance explicitly

TolX is disabled by default. Set
`with_absolute_distribution_size_tolerance` to compare
`sigma * max(d[i])` against a threshold in parameter units, where `d[i]`
are principal-axis standard deviations before multiplication by `sigma`.
`None` disables the check, zero requests exact collapse, and positive
thresholds use a strict inequality. A successful check reports
`CmaEsTolerance`.

This tests the unrestricted distribution's size. It does not measure a
projected gradient, a feasible error bound, or global optimality. Keep
iteration or evaluation budgets on the executor and inspect its report.
As with plain CMA-ES, other CMA stopping heuristics are not implemented.

## Implementation

`PopulationProgress` stores repaired members, raw costs, the repaired mean,
and the incumbent. The solver owns unrepaired genotypes, penalized fitness,
the distribution, penalty weights and history, and its seeded `ChaCha8Rng`.
`Executor::from_start` supplies the mean; explicitly constructed progress
can use `PopulationProgress::from_point`.

Retain the solver with `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect `mean()`,
`sigma()`, `distribution()`, `genotypes()`, and `penalized_costs()`.
Fresh initialization resets the distribution, penalty history, and RNG.
Exact solver-and-state checkpoints preserve them with progress and counts
and skip initialization; state-only snapshots start a new distribution.
The same seed reproduces a deterministic objective's trajectory within
the same backend.

Supported pairs are `Vec<F>`/`DenseMatrix<F>`,
`nalgebra::DVector<F>`/`DMatrix<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`/`Array2<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`/`Mat<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. All four dense paths
provide covariance eigendecomposition, diagonal extraction, and box repair
in pure Rust without a BLAS/LAPACK provider. Dense covariance storage and
factorization impose the same dimension costs as plain CMA-ES.

